                {
                    "id": "heading-1-1",
                    "type": "heading",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "🧬 Unlocking Irregular Verbs: History & Mechanics",
                        "level": 1,
                        "align": "left"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted"
                },
                {
                    "id": "paragraph-1-1",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "While regular verbs simply add <strong>-ed</strong> or <strong>-d</strong> to form the past tense (e.g. <em>walk → walked</em>), hundreds of the most common verbs in English do not follow this standard rule. These are <strong>Irregular Verbs</strong>. Their forms evolved from ancient Germanic speech patterns, mutating their internal vowels or consonants over centuries.",
                        "align": "left"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted"
                },
                {
                    "id": "poll-1-1",
                    "type": "poll",
                    "props": {
                        "question": "Which of these simple past forms is an IRREGULAR verb?",
                        "options": [
                            {
                                "id": "opt1",
                                "text": "Flew (from Fly)"
                            },
                            {
                                "id": "opt2",
                                "text": "Jumped (from Jump)"
                            },
                            {
                                "id": "opt3",
                                "text": "Looked (from Look)"
                            },
                            {
                                "id": "opt4",
                                "text": "Painted (from Paint)"
                            }
                        ],
                        "points": 5,
                        "state": "active"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted"
                },
                {
                    "id": "paragraph-1-2",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "Instead of memorizing hundreds of random words, linguists group irregular verbs into <strong>3 main mutation categories</strong>: 1) Vowel Changes, 2) Zero-Change Invariables, and 3) Total Stem Transformations.",
                        "align": "left"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted"
                },
